# Cursor Rule: /watch

When invoked as `/watch [run-dir]` (empty = newest `.devloop/run-*`). Read-only: never dispatch,
never merge, never kill.

- Enumerate lane ids from `lane-<id>.json`.
- Per lane report three sources side by side: `report-<id>.json` `.status` (the model's claim),
  `worker-<id>.exit` (the shell's receipt — `0` clean, `124` timeout, `125` never started, absent =
  nothing returned), and the tail of `worker-<id>.log`. **Never call a lane done from the report
  when the receipt is absent or non-zero.**
- Liveness: `python3 <skills dir>/dev-loop/scripts/job.py status --root <RUN>/jobs --json` →
  `absent | running | done | lost | forged`; exit 2 = failed/not done, 3 = lost/forged. Report
  `lost` explicitly (no receipt, pid gone, possible live grandchildren; kill by session id, not
  process group).
- Bounded waiting only and only when asked: `job.py wait --root <RUN>/jobs --id ID --budget N
  --interval N`. An exhausted budget is PARTIAL, never done.
- Antigravity-hosted runs only: if an NDJSON stream from `agy_session.py --events-out` exists, run
  `python3 <skills dir>/dev-loop/scripts/agy_monitor.py <stream> --once` and honour exit 2
  (`vacuous` / `no_result` / `stalled`); it also exits 2 with verdict `no_stream` when the file is
  absent, so read the printed verdict, not the code alone. A Cursor lane writes no such stream —
  no step-level view.
- Output one line per lane, then the anomalies. Cite `pos-<id>.log` and `neg-<id>.log` together;
  a negative control that never ran, or that passed, means unverified, not done.
